// `x / 0`. The last of the four to be one thing.
//
// Until v0.1.247 this was three behaviours and none of them was a message on the
// compiled side: the interpreter raised, the C backend emitted a bare `a / b`
// (integer division by zero is *undefined behaviour* in C — this machine's arm64
// quietly answered 0 and the program carried on, an x86-64 build of the same
// source raises SIGFPE), LLVM emitted `sdiv` (undefined in IR, so the optimizer
// is entitled to assume it never happens), and Wasm trapped with no output at all.
//
// So the same program could print a wrong answer, crash, or die silently,
// depending on which backend and which CPU. It went unnoticed because the harness
// compared stdout and the interpreter never reached the print.
//
// Execution mode: 4 backends, compared on exit status + stdout + failure message

let n = 17;
let d = 0;
let _ = print ("dividing " ++ show n ++ " by " ++ show d);
let _ = print (show (n / d));
let _ = print "unreachable";
0
